    - The -m flag means "run a module that comes built-in with Python."
    - venv is the module we want to run.
    - The second venv — This is an argument we're passing to the tool. It's the name we want to give the new virtual environment.
    - Python, run your venv tool, and create a virtual environment in a folder named venv.
2. Command: source venv/bin/activate
    - Turns ON the virtual environment you just created.
    - source — A shell command that means "read this file and run its contents in my current terminal session."
    - venv/bin/activate — The path to a small script Python created inside your venv folder. This script tells your terminal "from now on, when someone says python or pip, use the ones in this bubble, not the system-wide ones.
